# The doc-plan format: documentation generated from specs, with every sentence traced to a spec node [FL-DOC-PLAN]

- status: draft
- x-created: 2026-11-16
- scope: A document, the doc plan, states how one audience's human documentation is generated from a project's specs: which sections, in what order, from which spec nodes selected by query, in what voice and at what reading level, with which static assets and visuals. It also fixes the grammar the generated Markdown uses to point back at the spec nodes it came from, and what the generated FOR-LLMS.md must hold. Each audience has its own source spec and its own plan.
- boundary: This file defines the plan's nodes and keys, the citation and anchor grammar in generated Markdown, the style profile and its default banned patterns, the reading-level key and its check, the two forms of the visual node, and the shape of FOR-LLMS.md. It does not define the writer agent or the steps it follows, and it does not define the software-development extension. The format does not generate anything: a plan is data, and a program or an agent reads it.
- not-this: Not a template language and not a build system. Not a summary of the specs: the generated text is written by an agent, and the plan only says where each part comes from. Not specific to one project, one product or one language. Not a new core key table: the new keys belong to a domain profile, and until that profile is declared a strict document that uses them is told so.
- rationale: Documentation written from specs by hand or by a general-purpose agent drifts from them release by release, changes shape between releases, and mixes what the specs require with what someone remembered. A plan makes the structure a reviewable, versioned document, and the citations make every sentence auditable against its source.
- x-depends-on-spec: Spec 39 (FL-NORMATIVE-SOURCE) defines normative content, source statements and the normative_effective value that this format reads. Spec 40 (FL-PROFILES) defines the profile mechanism that declares this format's keys, id prefixes and document tag. Neither is restated here.
- x-authority: Requirement: the stakeholder's requests restated in the goals below, whose words are kept in [SRC-DP-STATEMENTS]. Each goal's x-whose says which parts are his and which are the authors'. The lint and banned-pattern severities, the heading level range, the language-tag and language-path conventions, the release state, the figures list of a gated plan and the shape of dp-survey-audiences are the authors' design and are decisions of this file's authors, not his words. That a survey recommends the audience documents a project should have is his request, in [SRC-DP-SURVEY]. The design, the key names, the grammar, the default banned-pattern list and the thresholds are the architects' judgement and are not derived from those words; each such choice is marked as a decision or as an open item. A requirement that carries x-origin is the authors' design as its x-origin says, and is not marked as a decision or as an open item. The product owner confirmed two readings of the request, that each audience has its own source spec and its own plan and that the generated FOR-LLMS.md stays in the LTS shape inside a Markdown file: see [SRC-DP-CONFIRM-READINGS].
- x-not-built: Everything in this file. No profile declares the keys, no lint implements the checks and no generator reads a plan yet ([PLAN-DP]). The default patterns of [BANNED-DEFAULTS] carry their data keys as x-dp-* until the profile ships, so this file lints without errors on the current engine.
- x-applies-itself: This file follows spec 39's rule. The stakeholder's words appear only in the nodes under [SRC-DP-STATEMENTS], a section marked non-normative, each with a locator. Every goal restates one or more of them in the authors' words and links to them. Where a goal also holds the authors' design, its x-whose says which part.
- see-also: [TRACE-DP], [PLAN-DP], [OPEN-DP-DEPENDENCIES], [OPEN-DP-PUBLISH-BEYOND-DOCS]

## Goal: a project's documentation is generated from its specs, with the same structure every release [GOAL-DP-GENERATED-FROM-SPECS]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G1
- x-goal: A project's human documentation is generated from its specs. The structure, and the sources of static content such as a logo, are defined once, in a document that is not the specs and not the output, and every release uses the same structure; the content changes release by release as the specs change.
- x-whose: The stakeholder's request, typed. The goal restates it; the words are in [SRC-DP-STRUCTURE] and [SRC-DP-SAME-STRUCTURE].
- restates: [SRC-DP-STRUCTURE], [SRC-DP-SAME-STRUCTURE]

## Goal: documentation is organised by audience, each audience with its own source spec and plan [GOAL-DP-BY-AUDIENCE]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G2
- x-goal: Documentation is organised by audience. For one project, installing and using the software is one spec and writing specs for it is another; each audience has its own source spec and its own doc plan, and the output is Markdown in the repository's docs folder.
- x-whose: The stakeholder's request, typed, and his confirmation of the reading that each audience has its own source spec and plan. The goal restates both; the words are in [SRC-DP-BY-AUDIENCE] and [SRC-DP-CONFIRM-READINGS].
- restates: [SRC-DP-BY-AUDIENCE], [SRC-DP-CONFIRM-READINGS]

## Goal: the generator reads the specs directly, and only their normative part is a source [GOAL-DP-SPEC-SOURCES]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G3
- x-goal: The source LTS files are read directly by the generator and not through a summary. Only the normative part of a spec is a source; what a stakeholder said, and the existing documents, are not.
- x-whose: The first sentence is the stakeholder's suggestion, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-READ-DIRECT]. The second sentence is the authors' design, resting on spec 39 and on the rule that an old document is evidence of what readers needed and not a source of claims; no source statement carries it.
- restates: [SRC-DP-READ-DIRECT]

## Goal: the reasons behind a project's design are drawn from its specs [GOAL-DP-WHY-FROM-SPECS]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G4
- x-goal: The explanation of why a project is built as it is comes from goals and requirements in its specs. Where a spec lacks them, they are recovered into the spec first, marked as recovered for the owner to confirm, and only then used by a document.
- x-whose: The stakeholder's request, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-WHY-IN-SPECS]. That recovered goals are marked recovered is the authors' design, since code shows what a program does and not why.
- restates: [SRC-DP-WHY-IN-SPECS]

## Goal: existing documents are surveyed, and what is valuable in them is kept [GOAL-DP-SURVEY-AND-FOLD]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G5
- x-goal: Before anything is replaced, the existing documents of a repository are surveyed and each gets a recommendation to keep, drop or fold, with a reason; the survey also recommends the audience documents the project should have. What is valuable in a document marked fold is carried into the generated documentation.
- x-whose: The stakeholder's request, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-SURVEY] (which includes the recommendation of installer, user and other audience documents), and his naming of the project's existing documents as the best test case, in [SRC-DP-WHY-IN-SPECS]. What the test case is a test of is the authors' reading, that it is the survey's acceptance input; his words do not say, and his confirmation is pending: [OPEN-DP-TEST-CASE-MEANING]. That test case is carried by [SCEN-DP-TEST-CASE-EXISTING-DOCS]. Where folded content lands is the authors' design: a reason becomes a goal or requirement in a spec, and a tutorial, example or walkthrough becomes an authored node of the plan.
- restates: [SRC-DP-SURVEY], [SRC-DP-WHY-IN-SPECS]
- see-also: [OPEN-DP-TEST-CASE-MEANING]

## Goal: every project gets a FOR-LLMS.md that serves two roles and stays in the LTS shape [GOAL-DP-FOR-LLMS]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G6
- x-goal: Every project documented this way gets a FOR-LLMS.md. One document lets an LLM learn what the software does and why, well enough to tell a human whether to use it and where to go for technical detail; and it holds what an agent needs to write install instructions or to install and validate the software. Generation never drops the install role. The file stays LTS-shaped inside a Markdown file.
- x-whose: The overview role, the install role and the file's present form are the stakeholder's words, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-EXISTS], [SRC-DP-AGENTS-PARSE-LTS], [SRC-DP-INSTALL-ROLE], [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-ROLE] and [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-COLD]. His test, that a reader given only the file can say how the software would or would not help a named project and where to go for detail, is in [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-COLD] and is carried by [SCEN-DP-FOR-LLMS-COLD-AGENT]. His confirmation that the file stays LTS-shaped is [SRC-DP-CONFIRM-READINGS]. That the install role is never dropped by generation is the authors' reading of [SRC-DP-INSTALL-ROLE].
- restates: [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-EXISTS], [SRC-DP-AGENTS-PARSE-LTS], [SRC-DP-INSTALL-ROLE], [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-ROLE], [SRC-DP-FOR-LLMS-COLD], [SRC-DP-CONFIRM-READINGS]

## Goal: the format serves any project and any language [GOAL-DP-ANY-PROJECT]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G7
- x-goal: Nothing in the format is specific to one project, product or language. The target languages are asked for and recorded, only English is produced for now, and language is a parameter throughout so that adding one later is a run and not a redesign.
- x-whose: The language rule is the stakeholder's request, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-LANGUAGES] and [SRC-DP-MULTILINGUAL]. That language is a parameter throughout, so that adding one is a run and not a redesign, is the authors' design (the default offered to him in the design session); he typed a different answer, which is [SRC-DP-LANGUAGES]. That nothing in the format may be specific to one project is the authors' inference from his two later turns, [SRC-DP-SECOND-CONSUMER] and [SRC-DP-FIRST-RUN-ON-OTHER-PRODUCT], which name a second product as the next consumer. Neither turn says any project.
- restates: [SRC-DP-LANGUAGES], [SRC-DP-MULTILINGUAL], [SRC-DP-SECOND-CONSUMER], [SRC-DP-FIRST-RUN-ON-OTHER-PRODUCT]

## Goal: the documentation has a chosen voice and a chosen reading level, and neither is a default assistant voice [GOAL-DP-STYLE-AND-LEVEL]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G8
- x-goal: A plan names a documentation style, for example corporate or casual, and a target reader education level, and records both. No style sounds like a general-purpose assistant's default voice.
- x-whose: The stakeholder's request, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-STYLES] and [SRC-DP-EDUCATION]. His turn gives corporate and casual as examples with a marker for the first word (eg), so the set of styles is open. The voice he excludes is a named model's; the elided name is a model's name, and the goal's wording, a general-purpose assistant's default voice, is the authors' reading. That the plan holds the reading level is the authors' reading of the reference file he names, and his confirmation is pending: [OPEN-DP-READING-LEVEL-HOME]. That a lint and a cold reader check the voice, that a readability score checks the level, and that those checks report as errors are the authors' design.
- restates: [SRC-DP-STYLES], [SRC-DP-EDUCATION]

## Goal: the generator makes the visuals it can make and recommends the ones it cannot [GOAL-DP-VISUALS]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G9
- x-goal: The generator creates the visuals a language-model instance can produce itself, such as diagrams and charts, and recommends where visuals should appear, including ones it cannot make. The format does not require a screenshot of every dialog or callouts on screenshots.
- x-whose: The stakeholder's request and its later clarification, typed, restated from [SRC-DP-VISUAL-RECOMMEND], [SRC-DP-VISUAL-CORRECTION] and [SRC-DP-VISUAL-LIMIT]. His limit is what a model instance could generate; the formats that satisfy it are the authors' reading, stated in [DD-DP-VISUAL-FORMATS-OPEN]. The citation of the nodes a visual depicts and the two forms of the node are the authors' design.
- restates: [SRC-DP-VISUAL-RECOMMEND], [SRC-DP-VISUAL-CORRECTION], [SRC-DP-VISUAL-LIMIT]

## Goal: each release can be reviewed as a diff, and a section keeps its identity across releases [GOAL-DP-REVIEWABLE-RELEASES]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-label: G10
- x-goal: Each release's documents can be reviewed as a diff against the previous release's, every changed section naming the spec nodes it came from, and a cold check of coverage and invention can run before the operator sees it. A section's identity is stable across releases.
- x-whose: Two parts. The diff against the previous release, each changed section naming the spec nodes it came from, with a cold audit of coverage and invention before the stakeholder sees it, and the definition of done as a newcomer installing and using the software from the generated documents alone, are the stakeholder's selection of two options the assistant wrote: [SRC-DP-SELECTED-DONE]. A selection is not his words. Paragraph-level citation and stable section ids are the authors' addition. His wish for the same structure release by release, in [SRC-DP-SAME-STRUCTURE], supports stable sections.
- restates: [SRC-DP-SELECTED-DONE], [SRC-DP-SAME-STRUCTURE]

## Source statements (non-normative) [SRC-DP-STATEMENTS]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- normative: false
- x-note: Everything in this section, and below it, is non-normative. It records what was said, as contiguous excerpts of the stakeholder's turns in one design session; the locator names the whole turn. An excerpt is verbatim, byte for byte from the transcript record, including its typing slips and its doubled spaces. Where a turn names a person, an agent, a model or a product of another project, the excerpt either stops short of the name, begins after it, or replaces the name with the marker [...], so that the elision is visible; no other word is replaced. It is never read as a requirement, an acceptance criterion or a test target.

