## Build order and rollout [PLAN-DP]

- part-of: [FL-DOC-PLAN]
- status: draft
- x-order: One change adds the domain profile (the keys reaching every consumer of a profile, per rule 13 of x-sdlc) that declares the dp- keys, the id prefixes and the document tag DOCPLAN, with the nodes of [BANNED-DEFAULTS] carried as the profile's data. The same change renames the default nodes' x-dp-* keys to dp-*; the rename is a spelling change and does not increment dp-banned-rev. The lint of [MECH-DP-LINT] follows, the plan checks first and the output checks after. A generator that reads a plan follows the lint, and it is not part of this format.
- x-before-use: No strict plan uses a dp- key before the profile lands, or it gets undeclared_key errors. This file is the first document of the format and carries the default-pattern data of [BANNED-DEFAULTS] as x-dp-* keys until the profile lands.
- x-depends: Spec 39 must be built before the always-on normative filter and the citation checks mean anything, because normative_effective is minted there.
- x-sdlc: In the same change, SDLC 7.6 gains a rule for each extension point of this format, drafted with the engine owner at build time: (1) a doc-plan section names its source nodes by query and never by a hand list; (2) a section id is never reused, and a rename carries a recorded map (superseded-by); (3) generation of FOR-LLMS.md preserves the install section; (4) a new dp-kind value adds its id prefix to the profile after a prior-use check across the registered corpora, with its lint rules and a scenario, in one change; (5) a change to the default banned-pattern list increments dp-banned-rev, which re-opens every plan; (6) a new find or traverse argument, where operator or match mode is added to this format's query terms, or listed there as not available, in the same change; (7) the plan-query resolver is an internal find caller and passes semantic off, word_fallback false and an unbounded token budget, and fails loudly on oversize_skipped; (8) any tool that reads or writes the anchor and citation comments imports the one grammar of the citation requirement, and a second pattern is a finding; (9) a new doc-plan finding code joins the lint's lists with its severity and a scenario in the same change; (10) a new way for a section to acquire a source (a query term, a citation form, a visual's cited ids, dp-also-from) applies the normative_effective filter, and a change to normative_effective is checked against this format; (11) a change to the form or scope of content_hash is checked against the release manifest and update-scope rules; (12) a new dp-reading-formula name lands with its formula, counting rules and scenarios together; (13) the banned-pattern defaults and the profile ship as package data, with a wheel test, and the profile's keys reach both profile loaders, the profile validator, SPEC.md and the profile audit in the one change. (14) a new named dp-visual-format joins the format list of the visual requirement with its check, its lint codes and a scenario in one change, and a value admitted only through dp-visual-check is never given a lint pass. (15) a new way for a section to acquire a source, or a new plan-level reference to a node, is covered by public_cites_private_node in the same change, with a scenario; (16) a feature that scans text against the deployment's policy calls the one matcher, defines no vocabulary of its own, never echoes matched text in a finding, and reports cannot-run as an error, never as a pass; (17) dp-publication and dp-text-scan never gain a default, and a new value of either lands with its lint behaviour, its release-record meaning and a scenario; (18) whether a corpus is private is decided only by the engine's one privacy rule (SPEC.md 4.6.4b), and a new condition goes into that rule and that section in the same change; (19) the x-dp-* to dp-* rename is one step of the profile change (x-order) and is checked after it by finding no property line in this file whose key begins x-dp- (zero hits).
- x-rollback: Remove the profile and the lint. A plan document then parses as ordinary properties, and a strict one reports undeclared_key; no data format changes.
- x-not-in-scope: The writer agent and its procedure, and the software-development extension. They read this format and are specified apart from it.

